"""18.05 Exam 2 practice problems: source-based edited solutions."""
ITEMS=[]
def add(n,q,a,sol):ITEMS.append((n,q,a,sol))
add(1,r"For standard normal $Z$: (a) Find (i) $P(Z<1.5)$, (ii) $P(Z>1.5)$, (iii) $P(-1.5<Z<1.5)$, (iv) $P(Z\le1.625)$. (b) Find (i) the right-tail region of probability 0.05, (ii) the symmetric two-tail region of total probability 0.2, (iii) the central 50% interval. Use normal tables and interpolation as needed.",r"(a) $0.9332,0.0668,0.8664,0.9479$ approximately. (b) $[1.64485,\infty)$; $(-\infty,-1.28155)\cup(1.28155,\infty)$; $[-0.67449,0.67449]$, approximately.",r"Use $\Phi(1.5)=0.9332$, complements, and symmetry for the first three probabilities. Averaging table entries $\Phi(1.60)=0.9452$ and $\Phi(1.65)=0.9505$ gives about $0.9479$. For (b), invert the cdf at $0.95$, $0.10/0.90$, and $0.25/0.75$. The source's rough interpolated boundaries are 1.64, $\pm1.275$, and $\pm0.675$; the stated values refine them.")
add(2,r"Use Student t tables. (a) Estimate (i) $P(T>1.6)$ for 3 degrees of freedom, (ii) $P(T<1.6)$ for 3 degrees, (iii) $P(-1.68<T<1.68)$ for 49 degrees, (iv) $P(-1.6<T<1.6)$ for 49 degrees. (b) Find (i) the right critical value for tail probability 0.05 with 8 degrees, (ii) the symmetric two-tail region of total probability 0.2 with 16 degrees, (iii) the central 50% interval with 20 degrees.",r"(a) Approximately $0.10,0.90,0.90,0.88$ by the source tables. (b) Approximately $1.86$; $(-\infty,-1.34)\cup(1.34,\infty)$; $[-0.7,0.7]$.",r"For 3 degrees of freedom the right 0.1 critical value is about 1.64, so at 1.6 the tail is slightly above 0.1 and the left tail slightly below 0.9. For 49 degrees, the right 0.05 boundary is near 1.68, giving central probability 0.9; interpolation at 1.6 gives about 0.88. For (b), look up right tails 0.05, 0.10, and 0.25 with the indicated degrees of freedom and reflect across zero for the left boundary. The central-50% boundary is approximately 0.687 rather than the rough table interpolation 0.7.")
add(3,r"Use chi-square tables. (a) Estimate (i) $P(X>1.6)$ for $X\sim\chi^2_3$, (ii) $P(X>20)$ for $X\sim\chi^2_{16}$. (b) Find (i) the right critical value with tail probability 0.05 for 8 degrees of freedom, (ii) an equal-tail two-sided rejection region of total probability 0.2 for 16 degrees.",r"(a) Approximately $0.6594$ and $0.2202$; rough source table interpolation gives $0.66,0.25$. (b) Approximately $15.51$ and $[0,9.31]\cup[23.54,\infty)$.",r"Find the right-tail probabilities by locating the given statistics in the specified rows and interpolating. The source's 0.25 for the second tail is a coarse estimate, not an exact value. For (b), use the right 0.05 quantile for 8 degrees, and the right 0.90 and 0.10 critical values for 16 degrees. These leave 0.1 in each tail.")
add(4,r"A random sample is $5,1,3,3,8$. Compute its sample mean, sample standard deviation, and median.",r"Mean $4$, sample standard deviation $\sqrt7$, median $3$.",r"The sum is 20 and there are 5 observations. The squared deviations from 4 sum to $1+9+1+1+16=28$; division by $5-1$ gives sample variance 7. Sorting to $1,3,3,5,8$ gives median 3.")
add(5,r"(a) A coin is tossed 100 times with 62 heads. Find the MLE of head probability $\theta$. (b) Generalize to $k$ heads in $n$ tosses.",r"(a) $0.62$. (b) $\hat\theta=k/n$, including the boundary cases $k=0,n$.",r"Apart from a constant, the likelihood is $\theta^k(1-\theta)^{n-k}$. Its log derivative is $k/\theta-(n-k)/(1-\theta)$, which vanishes at $k/n$ for $0<k<n$; the second derivative is negative. For all tails or all heads, the likelihood is maximized at 0 or 1, respectively. Substitute $k=62,n=100$ for (a).")
add(6,r"Let $y_1,\ldots,y_n$ be iid discrete uniform observations on $\{1,\ldots,N\}$, with unknown positive integer $N$. Find its MLE.",r"$\hat N=\max_i y_i$.",r"If $N<\max y_i$, the likelihood is zero. Otherwise it is $N^{-n}$, decreasing in $N$. Its maximum is therefore at the smallest admissible integer, the sample maximum.")
add(7,r"Let $x_1,\ldots,x_n$ be iid observations from the exponential density $\lambda e^{-\lambda x}$, $x\ge0$. Find the MLE of $\lambda$.",r"$\hat\lambda=1/\bar x=n/\sum_i x_i$, when the sample sum is positive.",r"The likelihood is $\lambda^n e^{-\lambda\sum x_i}$, with log derivative $n/\lambda-\sum x_i$. Setting it to zero gives the result, and the second derivative $-n/\lambda^2$ is negative. If the sample sum is zero, there is no finite maximizing rate.")
add(8,r"Observations $x_1,\ldots,x_n$ are iid geometric($1/a$), counting trials up to and including the first success. Thus the support is $1,2,\ldots$ and $a\ge1$. Find the MLE of $a$.",r"$\hat a=\bar x$.",r"The pmf is $(1-1/a)^{x_i-1}/a$. Write $S=\sum x_i$. The log likelihood is $(S-n)\ln(a-1)-S\ln a$. Its derivative is $(S-n)/(a-1)-S/a$, giving $a=S/n$. If every observation is one, the maximum is at the endpoint $a=1$.")
add(9,r"An MIT class has students numbered 1 through $n$, with unknown size $n$. Three distinct students selected uniformly without replacement have numbers $1,3,7$. Find the MLE of $n$.",r"$\hat n=7$.",r"The likelihood of the unordered set is zero for $n<7$ and $1/\binom n3$ for $n\ge7$. It is decreasing in the admissible range, so the maximum occurs at 7.")
add(10,r"One quarter of twin pairs are identical and three quarters fraternal. Assume identical twins have the same sex, while fraternal twins have independent equiprobable sexes. (a) Given that twins have the same sex, find the probability they are identical. (b) Obtain the posterior odds of identical twins by multiplying prior odds by a likelihood ratio, and check from (a).",r"(a) $2/5$. (b) Prior odds $1/3$, likelihood ratio 2, posterior odds $2/3$.",r"The same-sex likelihood is 1 for identical twins and $1/2$ for fraternal twins. Bayes' theorem gives $(1/4)/[(1/4)+(3/4)(1/2)]=2/5$. Prior odds $(1/4)/(3/4)=1/3$ multiplied by likelihood ratio $1/(1/2)=2$ gives $2/3$, agreeing with $(2/5)/(3/5)$.")
add(11,r"A drawer contains 4-, 6-, 8-, 12-, and 20-sided fair dice in proportions $1:2:10:2:1$. A friend selects one at random and rolls it twice, obtaining 5 both times. (a) Find the probability it is an eight-sided die. (b) Find posterior predictive probabilities that the next roll is (i) 5 and (ii) 15.",r"(a) Approximately $0.68472$. (b) Approximately $0.13179$ and $0.0005478$.",r"The four-sided hypothesis has likelihood zero. For the other dice the unnormalized posterior weights are $2/36,10/64,2/144,1/400$, whose sum is about $0.22819$. Normalize these to obtain posteriors $0.243457,0.684723,0.060864,0.010956$. For a next 5, sum posterior divided by the corresponding die size; for a next 15, only the twenty-sided die contributes, giving $0.010956/20$.")
add(12,r"Sameer chooses uniformly between a fair coin and a coin with head probability $3/4$, then flips until the first tail. The observations are HHHT. (a) Find prior and posterior odds for the fair coin. (b) Find prior and posterior predictive probabilities of heads on a subsequent flip of the same coin.",r"(a) Prior odds 1, posterior odds $16/27$. (b) Prior predictive $5/8$; posterior predictive $113/172\approx0.65698$.",r"The likelihood ratio for HHHT is $[(1/2)^3(1/2)]/[(3/4)^3(1/4)]=16/27$. Multiplying by prior odds one gives the posterior odds. Thus the posterior probabilities of fair and biased coins are $16/43,27/43$. The prior prediction is $(1/2)(1/2)+(1/2)(3/4)=5/8$; the posterior prediction is $(16/43)(1/2)+(27/43)(3/4)=113/172$.")
add(13,r"Peter and Jerry model the fraction $\theta$ of MIT 18.05 students preferring Bayesian statistics with prior $\operatorname{Beta}(2,2)$. Ten independent sampled students are polled, with $x_1$ preferring Bayesian statistics. (a) Give the conditional pmf of $x_1$. (b) Find the posterior for $x_1=6$. (c) Give equal-tail 50% and 90% posterior intervals, using R if needed. (d) The MAP is $7/12$; is there strong evidence that a majority prefer Bayesian statistics? (e) For an independent second sample of ten, write an integral for the posterior predictive probability of a Bayesian-preferring majority; do not evaluate.",r"(a) $x_1\mid\theta\sim\operatorname{Bin}(10,\theta)$. (b) $\operatorname{Beta}(8,6)$. (c) Approximately $[0.48330,0.66319]$, $[0.35480,0.77604]$. (d) Not strong; $P(\theta<1/2\mid x_1)=0.29053$. (e) $\int_0^1[\sum_{k=6}^{10}\binom{10}{k}\theta^k(1-\theta)^{10-k}]\frac{13!}{7!5!}\theta^7(1-\theta)^5\,d\theta$.",r"Multiplying the prior kernel $\theta(1-\theta)$ by likelihood $\theta^6(1-\theta)^4$ yields $\theta^7(1-\theta)^5$, a beta(8,6) kernel. Use qbeta at $(0.25,0.75)$ and $(0.05,0.95)$ for the intervals. The posterior still assigns about 29% below one half, so a MAP above one half is insufficient for a strong conclusion. For the prediction, integrate the conditional binomial upper tail against this normalized posterior.")
add(14,r"A bent coin has unknown head probability $\theta\in[0,1]$ with prior density $2(1-\theta)$. (a) Find its posterior after one tail. (b) Update again after a second tail. (c) On the same axes sketch the prior and both posterior densities.",r"(a) $3(1-\theta)^2$. (b) $4(1-\theta)^3$. All densities are zero outside $[0,1]$.",r"A tail has likelihood $1-\theta$. Multiplying once gives kernel $2(1-\theta)^2$, with integral $2/3$; normalizing gives $3(1-\theta)^2$. Multiplying that by the second-tail likelihood gives kernel $3(1-\theta)^3$, with integral $3/4$, so the next posterior is $4(1-\theta)^3$. The source plot below shows all three decreasing curves, with intercepts 2,3,4 and increasing concentration near zero.")
add(15,r"An experimental treatment cures an unknown fraction $\theta$ of patients. Use a uniform prior density on $[0,1]$. It cures 16 of 20 patients. Find the posterior density, writing an unevaluated integral $T$ for the normalizing probability of the data and expressing the posterior in terms of $T$.",r"$T=\int_0^1\binom{20}{16}\theta^{16}(1-\theta)^4d\theta$, and $f(\theta\mid\text{data})=\binom{20}{16}\theta^{16}(1-\theta)^4/T$ on $[0,1]$; a $\operatorname{Beta}(17,5)$ density.",r"The prior is constant and the likelihood is the binomial probability of 16 successes in 20 trials. Their product is $\binom{20}{16}\theta^{16}(1-\theta)^4$. Integrate this product to obtain the evidence $T$, then divide by $T$ to normalize. The exponents identify a beta(17,5) distribution.")
add(16,r"A cable has true length $\theta$. Each independent measurement has distribution $N(\theta,10^{-4})$. (a) With prior $\theta\sim N(9,1)$ and one observation $x=10$, find the posterior. (b) Using the same prior, find the smallest total number of measurements making posterior variance less than $10^{-6}$.",r"(a) $N(100009/10001,1/10001)$. (b) $100$ measurements.",r"Normal prior and likelihood precisions add. Prior precision is 1 and each measurement contributes $10^4$. After one measurement, posterior variance is $1/10001$ and mean $(9+10^4\cdot10)/10001$. After $n$ independent measurements the variance is $1/(1+10^4n)$. The inequality $1/(1+10^4n)<10^{-6}$ requires $n>99.9999$, so the smallest integer is 100.")
add(17,r"There are 49 independent observations with sample mean 6.25 and sample variance 12. Test $H_0:N(4,10^2)$ against $H_A:N(\mu,10^2)$, $\mu\ne4$. (a) Test at level 0.05 using normal tables. (b) Sketch the null density, rejection region, and the area determining the p-value.",r"$z=1.575$, two-sided $p\approx0.1152$; do not reject $H_0$. Rejection region $|z|>1.96$.",r"The model gives the population standard deviation 10, so use it rather than the sample variance. The statistic is $(6.25-4)/(10/7)=1.575$. Its null law is standard normal. The p-value is $2[1-\Phi(1.575)]\approx0.1152>0.05$. In the sketch the rejection tails start at $\pm1.96$ and the p-value tails at $\pm1.575$; the source figure is retained below.")
add(18,r"There are 49 independent normal observations with sample mean 6.25 and sample variance 36. Test $H_0:\mu=4$ against $H_A:\mu\ne4$, with unknown population variance. (a) Test at level 0.05 using a Student t table. (b) Sketch the null density, rejection region and p-value area.",r"$t=2.625$ with 48 degrees of freedom; $p\approx0.0116<0.05$, so reject. The two-sided critical values are approximately $\pm2.011$.",r"Use sample standard deviation 6. The statistic is $(6.25-4)/(6/7)=2.625$ and its null law is $t_{48}$. The two-sided p-value is $2P(t_{48}>2.625)\approx0.0116$, still below 0.05; the source's claim $p<0.01$ is inaccurate. The plot shades rejection tails beyond $\pm2.011$ and p-value tails beyond $\pm2.625$.")
# Problem 19 only points to other resources, and is not a self-contained problem.
